
# 1. Git Merge Conflict Resolution

## What is a Merge Conflict?
A merge conflict occurs when Git cannot automatically
combine changes from different branches.

It usually happens when two branches modify the same
part of a file or when conflicting file operations occur.

## Why Do Merge Conflicts Occur?
- Two developers modify the same line.
- One branch deletes a file while another modifies it.
- Different changes cannot be automatically combined.
- Changes overlap during a merge or rebase.

## Why is Merge Conflict Resolution Needed?
- To combine changes correctly.
- To prevent accidental loss of code.
- To maintain code quality.
- To ensure the application works correctly.

## How Does Merge Conflict Resolution Work?
1. Git identifies conflicting changes.
2. Git marks the conflicted files.
3. The developer opens the affected files.
4. The developer reviews both versions.
5. The developer selects or combines the required changes.
6. The developer stages the resolved files.
7. The merge operation is completed.



## Important Commands

git status
Shows files with merge conflicts.

git diff
Displays conflicting changes.

git add filename
Marks the file as resolved after editing.

git commit
Completes the merge when a merge commit is required.

git merge --abort
Cancels an ongoing merge and returns to the
pre-merge state.

## Real-World Example
Two developers work on the same application.
One updates the login functionality while another
changes the same section.

When their branches are merged, Git may report
a conflict. The team reviews both changes and
decides which code should be retained.



# 2. Git Stash and Stash Pop

## What is Git Stash?
Git stash temporarily saves uncommitted changes
so that developers can work on another task
without creating a commit.

It is useful when developers need to switch
branches or handle urgent work.

## Why is Git Stash Used?
- To temporarily save unfinished work.
- To switch branches safely.
- To work on urgent tasks.
- To keep the commit history clean.
- To restore work later.

## What is Git Stash Pop?
Git stash pop applies the most recently stashed
changes to the working directory and removes
that stash entry if the application succeeds.

## How Does Git Stash Work?
1. A developer makes changes to files.
2. The developer saves the changes using stash.
3. The working directory becomes clean for
   the saved tracked changes.
4. The developer works on another task.
5. The developer restores the saved changes later.

## Important Commands

git stash
Temporarily saves tracked changes.

git stash list
Displays all saved stash entries.

git stash pop
Applies the latest stash and removes its entry
if successful.

git stash apply
Applies saved changes but keeps the stash entry.

git stash drop
Deletes a selected stash entry.

git stash clear
Removes all stash entries.

git stash push -m "message"
Saves changes with a descriptive message.

## Difference Between Stash Pop and Stash Apply

Stash Pop:
Applies changes and removes the stash entry
if successful.

Stash Apply:
Applies changes but keeps the stash entry.

## Real-World Example
A developer is working on a new feature.
Suddenly, a critical bug needs to be fixed.

Instead of committing unfinished work, the developer
uses Git stash, switches to the required branch,
fixes the bug, and later restores the saved work.

## Important Point
Git stash is mainly used for temporary local changes.
Untracked files are not included by default.
Use git stash -u to include untracked files.

